AI Coding Assistant & Developer Rules

Section 0A: Core Philosophy & Communication Principles

Developer First: Focus on clear, maintainable, and idiomatic code.

Language standard: Communication, comments, and summary updates must be provided in Thai (ภาษาไทย) unless explicitly instructed otherwise.

Safety & Precision: Do not overwrite or modify existing critical code unless explicitly asked or required to solve the task.

Section 0B: Multi-Project Tree Isolation

Goal: Prevent AI from mixing up folders, sub-projects, or context in multi-repository or workspace setups.

Working Directory Verification:

Always verify the Current Working Directory (CWD) using system context or terminal commands (pwd / ls) before performing any file operations, reads, or modifications.

Strict Boundary Rules:

Do not touch, read, or execute files outside the target project folder.

If multiple projects exist in the workspace, explicitly log or verify which root folder is active before executing commands.

Ignore Patterns: Always ignore common cache and build output folders during file search and tree navigation:

node_modules/
.git/
dist/
build/
.next/
bin/
obj/
*.log


Section 1: Code Style & Quality Standards

Write modular, readable, and well-typed code (TypeScript/Typed languages preferred where applicable).

Keep functions small, focused, and compliant with Single Responsibility Principles (SRP).

Ensure error handling is robust, explicit, and logged appropriately.

Section 15: Environment & Git Sync Workflow

Goal: Maintain seamless multi-environment collaboration and code reliability across Local and Remote environments.

15.1 Development Workflows

At Home (Local-First Workflow):

Perform all code changes and run unit/integration tests locally.

Ensure test suites pass completely on the local environment.

Commit changes with clear, descriptive messages.

Push changes up to the Git remote repository.

Away / Remote (Remote-First Workflow):

Perform code updates or edits on the Remote server/environment.

Run tests to confirm functionality on the remote site.

Commit and push remote changes to Git.

Pull the latest commits back down to sync the Local machine before starting local work.

15.2 Post-Verification Auto Push & Summary Rule

Trigger: Once E2E tests, build checks, or test suites have passed successfully.

Action Required:

Update Thai Summary Documentation: Automatically compile and update a summary document or changelog in Thai detailing the work completed, tested features, and key changes.

Auto Stage & Commit: Stage all updated files (including source code and the newly updated Thai documentation summary).

Git Push: Execute git push immediately to keep remote and staging branches synchronized.